# standard: Go net/http path cleaning
# A request router for std/http: `Router#handle(pattern) { |req, res,
# params| ... }` registers a handler, and `router.call(req, res)` is what
# a `Server.answer` handler's `call` runs. The patterns, their precedence and
# the answers when none applies are Go's `http.ServeMux` - its 1.22
# patterns, its 1.26 redirects - implemented from its documentation
# (pkg.go.dev/net/http#ServeMux), so a table of routes reads the same in
# both.
#
# A pattern is `[METHOD ][HOST]/[PATH]`. A method matches exactly, except
# that `GET` matches `HEAD` too; no method matches every method, no host
# every host. A path is segments: a literal matches the request's segment
# percent-decoded (`/%61` matches `/a`, `/a%2fb` matches one segment
# `a/b`); `{name}` matches one non-empty segment; a last `{name...}`
# matches the rest of the path, slashes and nothing included; a trailing
# `/` is an anonymous one, a subtree; `{$}` matches only the end, so `/{$}`
# is `/` alone where `/` is every path. A wildcard's value is
# percent-decoded and handed to the handler by name in a
# `Hash(String, String)`; a trailing `/` records nothing.
#
# When several patterns match a request the most specific wins: the one
# whose requests are a strict subset of the other's. Two patterns neither
# of which is more specific, but which both match some request, conflict,
# and the second is refused when it is registered, with a sentence naming
# both and a request both match: `GET /` and `/index.html` conflict (one
# has the narrower method, the other the narrower path), as do `/a/{x}`
# and `/{y}/b`. A pattern with a host beats one without, whatever their
# paths, and the two never conflict - Go's one exception, kept for its
# reasons. Precedence is decided by comparing patterns, not by the order
# they were registered in, so a table means the same in any order.
#
# What no pattern answers: a path that matches only patterns of other
# methods - itself, or with a slash added - is 405 with `Allow` listing
# them, sorted, `HEAD` beside `GET`; a request for `/x` where `/x/` (or
# `/x/{rest...}`) is registered and `/x` is not is redirected to `/x/`
# (307, the query kept); a path with a `.` or `..` segment or a repeated
# slash is redirected to its cleaned form (307), as Go's is, so a handler
# is only ever handed the path its pattern matched; anything else is
# 404. An escaped `%2e%2e` or `%2F` is not a dot segment or a separator,
# and arrives in a wildcard's value as `..` or `/`: a handler that turns
# a value into a file name checks it (std/fileserver does its own).
#
# Both redirects are 307 Temporary Redirect, as Go's have been since 1.26;
# 1.22 to 1.25 answered 301 Moved Permanently. Go's reasons, which are
# this router's: a client may turn a POST it is sent on with a 301 into a
# GET, where a 307 is sent again with its method and body, and a client
# may keep a permanent redirect for good, where the redirect is the
# router's and not the program's, and a pattern registered in a later
# version of the program may answer that path itself. Go's release notes
# name the trailing-slash redirect, and its `server.go` answers the
# cleaned path with 307 as well; this follows the code. The status is
# fixed, not an option: a router that could answer either would need its
# tables checked twice, and the older answer is the one Go left.
#
# Where this departs from Go, and why: a path with a malformed percent
# escape is 400 here - Go's server refuses it before its mux sees it, and
# std/http hands it on. A pattern holding one, or whose path is not clean
# (`//`, `.`, `..`), is refused where Go 1.25 takes it as written: no
# request reaches it, cleaned and with its escapes valid. A target that
# is not a path (`OPTIONS *`, `CONNECT`'s authority) is 404. Wildcard
# names are ASCII identifiers (Go takes any Unicode letter). The `Host`
# is matched as Go matches it: its port dropped, otherwise as sent.
# `test/cli/std_router_exercise.sh` hands its tables - 70 requests, 23 pairs
# of patterns registered in turn, 28 patterns read - to Go's own
# `ServeMux` where a Go toolchain is installed. Under Go 1.26 or later
# every answer is the same; under 1.22 to 1.25 the one difference allowed
# is a redirect's 301 where this answers 307 with the same `Location`,
# and the gate names it.
#
# Patterns are registered before the router answers: a `handle` after its
# first `call` panics. Go takes them at any time under a lock; refusing
# them means the table a request is matched against is never written
# while a fiber reads it, and that whether two patterns conflict is known
# before any request depends on which wins. Each request is compared
# with every pattern of its host, which is linear in the table; there is
# no tree, since tables are tens of routes and the comparison that picks
# the winner is the same one that refuses a conflict, which keeps the
# two from disagreeing. Measured, a release build with the gate's first
# table (21 patterns) routed 200,000 requests in 165 ms, under a
# microsecond each.

@[NoSpec("std API predates spec-required; proofs follow")]
module std/router

import std/http::{HTTP, Incoming, Outgoing}
import std/uri::{URI}

# Why a pattern is refused: `Pattern.parse`'s answer for text that is not
# a pattern. `Router#handle` panics with its `message`.
@[OnTape]
pub struct RouterError
  getter reason : String

  def initialize(@reason : String)
  end

  def to_s : String
    "Router: " + @reason
  end
end

impl Error for RouterError
  def message : String
    "Router: " + @reason
  end
end

# A route's pattern, `[METHOD ][HOST]/[PATH]`, parsed: what `Router#handle`
# registers. `match` reads a request path's wildcards; `relation` and
# `conflict` are the comparisons that decide precedence and refuse two
# patterns neither of which is more specific.
@[OnTape]
pub struct Capture
  getter name : String
  getter value : String

  def initialize(@name : String, @value : String)
  end
end

@[OnTape]
pub struct Captures
  getter entries : Array(Capture)

  def initialize
    @entries = Array(Capture).new
  end

  def add(name : String, value : String) : Nil
    @entries << Capture.new(name, value)
  end

  def [](name : String) : String
    @entries.each { |entry| return entry.value if entry.name == name }
    raise "no route capture #{name}"
  end

  def to_h : Hash(String, String)
    result = {} of String => String
    @entries.each { |entry| result[entry.name] = entry.value }
    result
  end
end

@[OnTape]
pub struct Pattern
  # A path segment's kind: a literal, `{name}`, `{name...}` or a trailing
  # slash (with no name), and `{$}`.
  LITERAL = 0
  WILD    = 1
  MULTI   = 2
  ENDS    = 3

  # How the requests one pattern matches relate to another's: the same,
  # a strict superset, a strict subset, none in common, or some in common
  # and some not.
  EQUIVALENT    = 0
  MORE_GENERAL  = 1
  MORE_SPECIFIC = 2
  DISJOINT      = 3
  OVERLAPS      = 4

  getter method : String
  getter host : String
  @text : String
  # The path, a view of the text: its segments are read from it each time
  # (a literal percent-decoded as it is compared), so a Pattern holds no
  # data of its own and an Array(Pattern) is flat (SPEC 2.1).
  @path : String
  # How many segments the path has, and the kind of its last.
  @count : Int32
  @last : Int32

  protected def path : String
    @path
  end

  protected def count : Int32
    @count
  end

  protected def initialize(@text : String, @method : String, @host : String, @path : String, @count : Int32, @last : Int32)
  end

  # The pattern as it was written.
  def to_s : String
    @text
  end

  # *text* read as a pattern, or why it is not one: an invalid method, no
  # `/` to start the path, a `{` in the host, a path a request's cleaned
  # path never equals (`//`, `.`, `..`), a wildcard that is not a whole
  # segment, `{$}` or `{name...}` before the end, a name that is not an
  # identifier or is used twice, a malformed percent escape. The Pattern
  # views *text*.
  def self.parse(text : String) : Pattern | RouterError
    return refused(text, "it is empty") if text.empty?
    p = text
    n = text.bytesize
    # The method ends at the first space or tab; Go's reading, so a space
    # inside a path is a method that is not one.
    method = ""
    rest = text
    cut = 0
    while cut < n && p[cut] != 32_u8 && p[cut] != 9_u8
      cut = cut + 1
    end
    if cut < n
      method = text[0, cut]
      return refused(text, "#{method.inspect} is not a method") unless HTTP.token?(method)
      start = cut
      while start < n && (p[start] == 32_u8 || p[start] == 9_u8)
        start = start + 1
      end
      rest = text[start, n - start]
    end
    slash = rest.index('/')
    return refused(text, "it has no path, which starts with /") unless slash
    host = rest[0, slash]
    return refused(text, "its host #{host.inspect} holds a { (is the / before it missing?)") if host.includes?("{")
    path = rest[slash, rest.bytesize - slash]
    # A request's path is cleaned before it is matched, so a pattern whose
    # path is not clean could never match one.
    cleaned = Router.clean(path)
    return refused(text, "its path is not clean: a request for it is sent to #{cleaned.inspect}") unless cleaned == path
    size = path.bytesize
    count = 0
    last = LITERAL
    at = 0
    while at < size
      stop = Pattern.segment_end(path, at)
      kind = Pattern.kind_of(path, at, stop)
      segment = path[at + 1, stop - at - 1]
      if kind == LITERAL
        return refused(text, "a wildcard is a whole segment, {name}, and #{segment.inspect} is not one") if segment.includes?("{")
        return refused(text, "#{segment.inspect} holds a malformed percent escape") unless Pattern.escapes_ok?(segment)
      elsif kind != MULTI || stop > at + 1
        return refused(text, "a wildcard is a whole segment, {name}, and #{segment.inspect} is not one") unless segment.ends_with?("}")
        name = segment[1, segment.bytesize - 2]
        return refused(text, "{$} is not at the end") if kind == ENDS && stop != size
        if kind != ENDS
          name = name[0, name.bytesize - 3] if kind == MULTI
          return refused(text, "{#{name}...} is not at the end") if kind == MULTI && stop != size
          return refused(text, "a wildcard has no name") if name.empty?
          return refused(text, "#{name.inspect} is not a wildcard name (a letter or _, then letters, digits and _)") unless Pattern.identifier?(name)
          return refused(text, "two wildcards are named #{name.inspect}") if Pattern.named_before?(path, at, name)
        end
      end
      count = count + 1
      last = kind
      at = stop
    end
    Pattern.new(text, method, host, path, count, last)
  end

  # Where the segment starting at the `/` at *at* of *path* ends: the next
  # `/` or the end; a trailing slash ends at once.
  protected def self.segment_end(path : String, at : Int32) : Int32
    size = path.bytesize
    stop = at + 1
    return stop if stop == size
    while stop < size && path[stop] != 47_u8
      stop = stop + 1
    end
    stop
  end

  # The kind of the segment between *at* and *stop*: a trailing slash is
  # MULTI; `{$}`, `{name...}`, `{...}` by their braces; else a literal.
  protected def self.kind_of(path : String, at : Int32, stop : Int32) : Int32
    return MULTI if at + 1 == path.bytesize
    return LITERAL unless path[at + 1] == 123_u8
    length = stop - at - 1
    return ENDS if length == 3 && path[at + 2] == 36_u8 && path[at + 3] == 125_u8
    return MULTI if length >= 5 && path[stop - 1] == 125_u8 && path[stop - 2] == 46_u8 && path[stop - 3] == 46_u8 && path[stop - 4] == 46_u8
    WILD
  end

  # The segment's text: a literal still encoded, a wildcard's name, ""
  # for a trailing slash and `{$}`. A view of *path*.
  protected def self.name_of(path : String, at : Int32, stop : Int32, kind : Int32) : String
    length = stop - at - 1
    return path[at + 1, length] if kind == LITERAL
    return path[at + 2, length - 2] if kind == WILD
    return path[at + 2, length - 5] if kind == MULTI && length > 0
    ""
  end

  # Whether a wildcard before *upto* in *path* is named *name*.
  protected def self.named_before?(path : String, upto : Int32, name : String) : Bool
    at = 0
    while at < upto
      stop = Pattern.segment_end(path, at)
      kind = Pattern.kind_of(path, at, stop)
      return true if (kind == WILD || kind == MULTI) && Pattern.name_of(path, at, stop, kind) == name
      at = stop
    end
    false
  end

  # Whether every `%` in *segment* starts a two-digit hex escape.
  protected def self.escapes_ok?(segment : String) : Bool
    i = 0
    size = segment.bytesize
    while i < size
      if segment[i] == 37_u8
        return false if i + 2 >= size
        return false if hex_value(segment[i + 1]) < 0 || hex_value(segment[i + 2]) < 0
        i += 3
      else
        i += 1
      end
    end
    true
  end

  # The byte of the encoded *raw* at *i* decoded, and where the next one
  # starts; *raw*'s escapes were checked by `parse`.
  protected def self.decoded_at(raw : String, i : Int32) : {UInt8, Int32}
    return {raw[i], i + 1} unless raw[i] == 37_u8
    {(hex_value(raw[i + 1]) * 16 + hex_value(raw[i + 2])).to_u8, i + 3}
  end

  # Whether the encoded literal *raw* decodes to *decoded*.
  protected def self.decodes_to?(raw : String, decoded : String) : Bool
    i = 0
    j = 0
    while i < raw.bytesize
      return false if j >= decoded.bytesize
      byte, i = Pattern.decoded_at(raw, i)
      return false unless byte == decoded[j]
      j += 1
    end
    j == decoded.bytesize
  end

  # Whether two encoded literals decode to the same text.
  protected def self.same_literal?(a : String, b : String) : Bool
    i = 0
    j = 0
    while i < a.bytesize && j < b.bytesize
      x, i = Pattern.decoded_at(a, i)
      y, j = Pattern.decoded_at(b, j)
      return false unless x == y
    end
    i == a.bytesize && j == b.bytesize
  end

  private def self.refused(text : String, reason : String) : RouterError
    RouterError.new("#{text.inspect} is not a pattern: #{reason}")
  end

  # A wildcard's name: an ASCII letter or `_`, then letters, digits, `_`.
  def self.identifier?(name : String) : Bool
    p = name
    i = 0
    while i < name.bytesize
      c = p[i]
      letter = (c >= 97_u8 && c <= 122_u8) || (c >= 65_u8 && c <= 90_u8) || c == 95_u8
      return false unless letter || (i > 0 && c >= 48_u8 && c <= 57_u8)
      i = i + 1
    end
    name.bytesize > 0
  end

  # Whether a request of *method* is one this pattern's method takes.
  def matches_method?(method : String) : Bool
    @method.empty? || @method == method || (@method == "GET" && method == "HEAD")
  end

  # Whether *path* - a request path, rooted and clean, still
  # percent-encoded - matches this pattern's path. The method and host are not looked at. The path's segments go
  # into the empty *pieces* and *text* as `pieces` writes them; the
  # wildcards' values, viewing them, are added to *found*. False when it
  # does not match: *found* may then hold some.
  def match(path : String, pieces : Array(String), text : String::Builder, found : Captures?) : Bool
    return false unless Pattern.pieces(path, pieces, text)
    match_pieces(pieces, text.to_s, found)
  end

  # *path*'s segments after its first `/`, each percent-decoded: `/` is
  # one empty piece, and a trailing slash is a last empty one. Nil for a
  # path that is not rooted or holds a malformed escape. Decoded a segment
  # at a time, so `%2F` is inside a segment, never between two.
  # The pieces are appended to *into*, viewing *text*, an empty builder
  # where they are written decoded and joined by `/`. False when *path*
  # is not rooted or holds a malformed escape.
  def self.pieces(path : String, into : Array(String), text : String::Builder) : Bool
    return false unless path.starts_with?("/")
    size = path.bytesize
    at = 0
    while at < size
      stop = at + 1
      while stop < size && path[stop] != 47_u8
        stop = stop + 1
      end
      segment = path[at + 1, stop - at - 1]
      text << "/" if at > 0
      start = text.length
      return false unless URI.decode?(segment, text)
      into << text.to_s[start, text.length - start]
      at = stop
    end
    true
  end


  # `match` on a path already cut into `pieces`, whose text is *joined*.
  # The wildcards' values go to *found*, unless it is nil.
  def match_pieces(pieces : Array(String), joined : String, found : Captures?) : Bool
    size = @path.bytesize
    at = 0
    j = 0
    # Whether the pattern took the whole path: a `{name...}` or trailing
    # slash takes the rest, slashes and all, nothing included - but not
    # past the end: `/x/` does not match `/x`.
    whole = false
    while at < size && !whole
      stop = Pattern.segment_end(@path, at)
      kind = Pattern.kind_of(@path, at, stop)
      name = Pattern.name_of(@path, at, stop, kind)
      return false if j >= pieces.size
      piece = pieces[j]
      if kind == MULTI
        # The rest is the end of *joined* past the pieces before it.
        offset = 0
        k = 0
        while k < j
          offset += pieces[k].bytesize + 1
          k += 1
        end
        found.add(name, joined[offset, joined.bytesize - offset]) if found && !name.empty?
        whole = true
      elsif kind == ENDS
        return false unless piece.empty? && j == pieces.size - 1
      elsif kind == WILD
        return false if piece.empty?
        found.add(name, piece) if found
      else
        return false unless Pattern.decodes_to?(name, piece)
      end
      at = stop
      j = j + 1
    end
    whole || j == pieces.size
  end

  private def self.hex_value(byte : UInt8) : Int32
    return byte.to_i32 - 48 if byte >= 48_u8 && byte <= 57_u8
    return byte.to_i32 - 87 if byte >= 97_u8 && byte <= 102_u8
    return byte.to_i32 - 55 if byte >= 65_u8 && byte <= 70_u8
    -1
  end

  # Whether this pattern matched *pieces* without its trailing wildcard
  # taking anything: `/x/` on `/x/`, not on `/x/y`. What decides whether
  # `/x` is redirected to `/x/`.
  def exact?(pieces : Array(String)) : Bool
    return true unless @last == MULTI
    pieces.size == @count && pieces.last.empty?
  end

  # Whether this pattern matches a strict subset of *other*'s requests,
  # methods and paths together; hosts are not compared.
  def more_specific?(other : Pattern) : Bool
    relation(other) == MORE_SPECIFIC
  end

  # How this pattern's requests relate to *other*'s, methods and paths
  # together: `EQUIVALENT`, `MORE_GENERAL`, `MORE_SPECIFIC`, `DISJOINT` or
  # `OVERLAPS`.
  def relation(other : Pattern) : Int32
    methods = compare_methods(other)
    return DISJOINT if methods == DISJOINT
    Pattern.combine(methods, compare_paths(other))
  end

  # The sentence refusing this pattern beside *other*, nil when the two
  # can be registered together: when they have the same host and match
  # the same requests, or some requests in common with neither more
  # specific. It names both and a request both match.
  def conflict(other : Pattern) : String?
    return nil unless @host == other.host
    methods = compare_methods(other)
    return nil if methods == DISJOINT
    paths = compare_paths(other)
    rel = Pattern.combine(methods, paths)
    return nil unless rel == EQUIVALENT || rel == OVERLAPS
    head = "Router: #{@text.inspect} conflicts with #{other.to_s.inspect}"
    return head + ": both match the same requests" if rel == EQUIVALENT
    verb = @method
    verb = other.method if verb.empty? || (verb == "GET" && !other.method.empty?)
    request = (verb.empty? ? "" : verb + " ") + common_path(other).inspect
    if paths == OVERLAPS
      head + ": both match #{request}, and neither is more specific than the other"
    elsif methods == MORE_GENERAL
      head + ": it matches more methods but a more specific path, and both match #{request}"
    else
      head + ": it matches fewer methods but a more general path, and both match #{request}"
    end
  end

  private def compare_methods(other : Pattern) : Int32
    return EQUIVALENT if @method == other.method
    return MORE_GENERAL if @method.empty?
    return MORE_SPECIFIC if other.method.empty?
    return MORE_GENERAL if @method == "GET" && other.method == "HEAD"
    return MORE_SPECIFIC if @method == "HEAD" && other.method == "GET"
    DISJOINT
  end

  private def compare_paths(other : Pattern) : Int32
    theirs = other.path
    their_count = other.count
    mine_multi = @last == MULTI
    theirs_multi = other.last_kind == MULTI
    # Without a trailing wildcard a path matches only paths of its own
    # number of segments.
    return DISJOINT if @count != their_count && !mine_multi && !theirs_multi
    rel = EQUIVALENT
    at = 0
    their_at = 0
    while at < @path.bytesize && their_at < theirs.bytesize
      stop = Pattern.segment_end(@path, at)
      their_stop = Pattern.segment_end(theirs, their_at)
      kind = Pattern.kind_of(@path, at, stop)
      their_kind = Pattern.kind_of(theirs, their_at, their_stop)
      rel = Pattern.combine(rel, compare_segments(kind, Pattern.name_of(@path, at, stop, kind), their_kind, Pattern.name_of(theirs, their_at, their_stop, their_kind)))
      return rel if rel == DISJOINT
      at = stop
      their_at = their_stop
    end
    return rel if @count == their_count
    # The shorter can still match the longer's paths if it ends in a
    # wildcard that takes the rest.
    return Pattern.combine(rel, MORE_GENERAL) if @count < their_count && mine_multi
    return Pattern.combine(rel, MORE_SPECIFIC) if their_count < @count && theirs_multi
    DISJOINT
  end

  protected def last_kind : Int32
    @last
  end

  # One segment beside the one at the same place in another pattern.
  private def compare_segments(kind : Int32, name : String, their_kind : Int32, their_name : String) : Int32
    return EQUIVALENT if kind == MULTI && their_kind == MULTI
    return MORE_GENERAL if kind == MULTI
    return MORE_SPECIFIC if their_kind == MULTI
    return EQUIVALENT if kind == WILD && their_kind == WILD
    # `{x}` never matches the end that `{$}` matches: a segment is not empty.
    return (their_kind == ENDS ? DISJOINT : MORE_GENERAL) if kind == WILD
    return (kind == ENDS ? DISJOINT : MORE_SPECIFIC) if their_kind == WILD
    return DISJOINT unless kind == their_kind
    kind == ENDS || Pattern.same_literal?(name, their_name) ? EQUIVALENT : DISJOINT
  end

  # Two relations of parts (methods, then each segment) as the relation
  # of the whole: a superset in one part and a subset in another is
  # neither, an overlap.
  def self.combine(first : Int32, second : Int32) : Int32
    case first
    when EQUIVALENT
      second
    when DISJOINT
      DISJOINT
    when OVERLAPS
      second == DISJOINT ? DISJOINT : OVERLAPS
    else
      return first if second == EQUIVALENT
      inverse = first == MORE_GENERAL ? MORE_SPECIFIC : MORE_GENERAL
      second == inverse ? OVERLAPS : second
    end
  end

  # A path both patterns match, for the sentence that refuses them: each
  # segment the literal of whichever has one, else a wildcard by its name
  # (a trailing wildcard taking nothing), and what the longer has left
  # after the shorter's trailing wildcard.
  private def common_path(other : Pattern) : String
    theirs = other.path
    text = String::Builder.new
    at = 0
    their_at = 0
    while at < @path.bytesize || their_at < theirs.bytesize
      if at < @path.bytesize
        stop = Pattern.segment_end(@path, at)
        mine = Pattern.kind_of(@path, at, stop)
      else
        stop = at
        mine = -1
      end
      if their_at < theirs.bytesize
        their_stop = Pattern.segment_end(theirs, their_at)
        their_kind = Pattern.kind_of(theirs, their_at, their_stop)
      else
        their_stop = their_at
        their_kind = -1
      end
      if mine < 0 || (their_kind >= 0 && (mine == MULTI || (mine == WILD && their_kind != MULTI)))
        Pattern.write_segment(text, their_kind, Pattern.name_of(theirs, their_at, their_stop, their_kind))
      else
        Pattern.write_segment(text, mine, Pattern.name_of(@path, at, stop, mine))
      end
      at = stop
      their_at = their_stop
    end
    text.to_s
  end

  # A segment of a request both match: a literal decoded, a wildcard by
  # its name.
  protected def self.write_segment(text : String::Builder, kind : Int32, name : String) : Nil
    text << "/"
    if kind == LITERAL
      URI.decode?(name, text)
    elsif kind == WILD
      text << name
    end
  end
end

# Requests sent to handlers by pattern, Go 1.22's `ServeMux`:
#
#     router = Router.new
#     router.handle("GET /users/{id}") { |req, res, params| res.body << params["id"] }
#     # Files: FileServer.serve(root, req, res) inside a handler.
#     # Build this router inside Handler.new(copied_config), and in
#     # Handler#call(req, res) run @router.call(req, res).
#     Server.answer("0.0.0.0", 3000, config, Handler)
#
# `handle` panics on a pattern that is not one, on one that conflicts with
# a pattern already registered, and after the router has answered a
# request. `call` answers with the most specific pattern's handler, or
# writes a 307 (to the cleaned path, or to a subtree's `/`), a 405 with
# `Allow`, a 400 for a malformed escape, a 404 into *res*.
pub class Router
  @patterns : Array(Pattern)
  @handlers : Array(Proc(Incoming, Outgoing, Hash(String, String), Nil))
  @answering : Bool

  def initialize
    @patterns = [] of Pattern
    @handlers = [] of Proc(Incoming, Outgoing, Hash(String, String), Nil)
    @answering = false
  end

  # Sends the requests *pattern* matches to the block, with the request,
  # the answer to write and the wildcards' values by name.
  def handle(pattern : String, &handler : Incoming, Outgoing, Hash(String, String) -> Nil) : Nil
    raise "Router: #{pattern.inspect} is registered after the router answered a request; patterns are registered before it serves" if @answering
    parsed = Pattern.parse(pattern).or_panic
    @patterns.each do |known|
      if sentence = parsed.conflict(known)
        raise sentence
      end
    end
    @patterns << parsed
    @handlers << handler
  end

  # The patterns registered, in the order they were.
  def patterns : Array(Pattern)
    @patterns.dup
  end

  # The answer to *req*, written into *res*: its handler's, or the
  # router's own 307, 400, 404 or 405.
  def call(req : Incoming, res : Outgoing) : Nil
    @answering = true
    raw = req.path
    # What answers, decided before anything is written into *res*: the
    # handler is called first, as nothing has touched *res* yet.
    # 0 the handler, 1 a 404, 2 a 400, 3 a 307 to the path and a slash,
    # 4 a 307 to the cleaned path, 5 a 405 or a 404.
    action = 5
    found = -1
    path = raw
    host = ""
    pieces = [] of String
    text = String::Builder.new(raw.bytesize + 1)
    if !raw.starts_with?("/")
      action = 1
    elsif !URI.decode?(raw)
      action = 2
    else
      path = Router.clean(raw)
      host = Router.host_of(req.header("Host") || "")
      Pattern.pieces(path, pieces, text)
      found = find(host, req.method, pieces, text.to_s)
      # `/x` where only `/x/`'s subtree would take it - or nothing would -
      # is sent to `/x/` when `/x/` itself is a request a pattern matches
      # exactly.
      exact = found >= 0 ? @patterns[found].exact?(pieces) : false
      unless exact || path.ends_with?("/")
        slashed = [] of String
        more = String::Builder.new(raw.bytesize + 1)
        Pattern.pieces(path + "/", slashed, more)
        again = find(host, req.method, slashed, more.to_s)
        action = 3 if again >= 0 && @patterns[again].exact?(slashed)
      end
      if action == 5
        if path != raw
          action = 4
        elsif found >= 0
          action = 0
        end
      end
    end
    if action == 0
      params = {} of String => String
      got = Captures.new
      @patterns[found].match_pieces(pieces, text.to_s, got)
      got.entries.each { |entry| params[entry.name] = entry.value }
      @handlers[found].call(req, res, params)
      return
    end
    return Router.plain(res, 404, "not found\n") if action == 1
    return Router.plain(res, 400, "bad request\n") if action == 2
    return Router.redirect(res, path + "/", req.query) if action == 3
    return Router.redirect(res, path, req.query) if action == 4
    allowed = allowed(host, path, pieces, text.to_s)
    unless allowed.empty?
      res.status = 405
      res.header("Allow", allowed.join(", "))
      res.header("Content-Type", "text/plain; charset=utf-8")
      res.body << "method not allowed\n"
      return
    end
    Router.plain(res, 404, "not found\n")
  end

  # The index of the pattern that takes the request: of the host's
  # own patterns if one matches, else of those with no host, the one more
  # specific than every other that matches; -1 when none does.
  private def find(host : String, method : String, pieces : Array(String), joined : String) : Int32
    unless host.empty?
      found = best(host, method, pieces, joined)
      return found if found >= 0
    end
    best("", method, pieces, joined)
  end

  private def best(host : String, method : String, pieces : Array(String), joined : String) : Int32
    chosen = -1
    i = 0
    while i < @patterns.size
      pattern = @patterns[i]
      # Of two that match, one is more specific: registration refused
      # every pair for which that is not so.
      if pattern.host == host && pattern.matches_method?(method) && (chosen < 0 || pattern.more_specific?(@patterns[chosen]))
        chosen = i if pattern.match_pieces(pieces, joined, nil)
      end
      i = i + 1
    end
    chosen
  end

  # The methods of the patterns whose host and path match, sorted, with
  # `HEAD` where `GET` is: the 405's `Allow`. As Go's, the path with a
  # slash added counts too - the request the redirect above would have
  # been, had the method matched - so `POST /x` beside `GET /x/` is 405
  # rather than 404.
  private def allowed(host : String, path : String, pieces : Array(String), joined : String) : Array(String)
    slashed = [] of String
    text = String::Builder.new(path.bytesize + 1)
    Pattern.pieces(path + "/", slashed, text) unless path.ends_with?("/")
    methods = [] of String
    @patterns.each do |pattern|
      next if pattern.method.empty? || methods.includes?(pattern.method)
      next unless pattern.host.empty? || pattern.host == host
      if pattern.match_pieces(pieces, joined, nil) || (!slashed.empty? && pattern.match_pieces(slashed, text.to_s, nil))
        methods << pattern.method
      end
    end
    methods << "HEAD" if methods.includes?("GET") && !methods.includes?("HEAD")
    methods.sorted
  end

  # *path* - rooted - with `.` and empty segments dropped and `..` taking
  # the one before it off, its trailing slash kept: Go's `cleanPath`.

  def self.clean(path : String) : String
    String.build { |w| clean(path, w) }
  end

  def self.clean(path : String,
                 writer : String::Builder) : Nil
    kept = Array(String).new
    path.split('/').each do |segment|
      if segment == ".."
        kept.pop?
      elsif !segment.empty? && segment != "."
        kept << segment
      end
    end
    writer << "/"
    kept.join(writer, "/")
    writer << "/" if path.bytesize > 0 && path[path.bytesize - 1] == 47_u8 &&
                     !kept.empty?
    nil
  end

  # A `Host` value without its port, as Go's `stripHostPort` has it: an
  # IPv6 address in brackets loses the brackets with the port.
  def self.host_of(value : String) : String
    if value.starts_with?("[")
      close = value.index(']')
      return value unless close && close + 1 < value.bytesize && value[close + 1] == 58_u8
      return value[1, close - 1]
    end
    colon = value.index(':')
    return value unless colon
    rest = value[colon + 1, value.bytesize - colon - 1]
    rest.index(':') ? value : value[0, colon]
  end

  # A 307 to *path* and *query*, written into *res*.
  def self.redirect(res : Outgoing, path : String, query : String?) : Nil
    res.status = 307
    res.header("Location") do |w|
      w << path
      if (q = query) && !q.empty?
        w << "?" << q
      end
    end
  end

  # *status* with *text* as a plain body, written into *res*.
  def self.plain(res : Outgoing, status : Int32, text : String) : Nil
    res.status = status
    res.header("Content-Type", "text/plain; charset=utf-8")
    res.body << text
  end
end

spec 8.1  # A std module follows the standard its header names (an RFC, a file…


  kept = ""
  path = "/a//b/../c/./"
  w = String::Builder.new(1)
  w << "prefix:"
  Router.clean(path, w)
  w.finish == "prefix:/a/c/"
  kept = Router.clean(path).copy_to(Program.tape)
  Router.clean("/../../") == "/"
  Router.clean("café/../ok") == "/ok"
  kept == "/a/c/"
  Router.clean("/a//b/../c/./") == kept
end

# router.3 Parsing returns Pattern or RouterError. It refuses invalid methods,
#   missing paths, host braces, unclean paths, bad percent escapes,
#   partial-segment wildcards, nonfinal rest or end wildcards, duplicate names
#   and names outside ASCII identifiers.
spec router.3
  hosted = Pattern.parse("PUT example.com/b/{bucket}/o/{rest...}").or_panic
  hosted.method == "PUT" && hosted.host == "example.com"
  hosted.to_s == "PUT example.com/b/{bucket}/o/{rest...}"
  p1 = [] of String
  t1 = String::Builder.new
  got = Captures.new
  hosted.match("/b/b%201/o/a/b/", p1, t1, got)
  got["rest"] == "a/b/" && got["bucket"] == "b 1"
  p2 = [] of String
  t2 = String::Builder.new
  !hosted.match("/b/x/o", p2, t2, nil)
  escaped = Pattern.parse("/a%2Fb/{c}").or_panic
  p3 = [] of String
  t3 = String::Builder.new
  escaped.match("/a%2fb/x", p3, t3, nil)
  p4 = [] of String
  t4 = String::Builder.new
  !escaped.match("/a/b/x", p4, t4, nil)
  Pattern.parse("/a%2Fb").or_panic.relation(Pattern.parse("/a%2fb").or_panic) == Pattern::EQUIVALENT
  Pattern.parse("/a/").or_panic.relation(Pattern.parse("/a/{x...}").or_panic) == Pattern::EQUIVALENT
  Pattern.parse("/{x}/{x}").is_a?(RouterError)
  Pattern.parse("/{x}/{y...}/z").is_a?(RouterError)
  Pattern.parse("/a%zz").is_a?(RouterError)
  Pattern.parse("/a{x}").is_a?(RouterError)
  Pattern.parse("/{$}/a").is_a?(RouterError)
  Pattern.parse("/{...}").is_a?(RouterError)
  Pattern.parse("G@T /a").is_a?(RouterError)
  Pattern.parse("/{1x}").is_a?(RouterError)
  Pattern.parse("/{a}/{b}/{$}").is_a?(Pattern)
end
